Run:  python3 book_demo.py              (prints the whole book)
      python3 book_demo.py 3            (prints chapter 3 only)
      python3 book_demo.py search WORD  (finds WORD across chapters)
      python3 book_demo.py html         (writes book.html)
"""
import json, sys, html as _h

BOOK = {'title': 'The Last Honest Man', 'author': 'Signature original', 'genre': 'Femme Fatales', 'chapters': [{'n': 1, 'title': 'The Client', 'text': "'I need you to find out what this means,' the client said, laying a broken watch on the desk like a bet. Vince Corso had seen a hundred such objects. They all meant the same thing: somebody was lying, and somebody was going to bleed.\n\nVince Corso took the case. Not for the money — the money was insulting — but because a broken watch had a look Vince Corso recognized: the look of the one honest thing in a dirty town.\n\nThat night Vince Corso stood at the office window, watching the neon bleed into the wet street. a broken watch sat in the safe. Outside, Harbor District did what it always did: it waited."}, {'n': 2, 'title': 'The Streets Talk', 'text': "The address led to Harbor District's underbelly — a locker, a ledger, a man who didn't want to talk until Vince Corso showed him a broken watch. Then he talked like a river, and Vince Corso wrote it all down.\n\nBack at the office, Vince Corso spread the pieces out: a broken watch, the ledger page, Ruby's note. The shape of it was the corrupt councilman, big as a building. And Vince Corso was just one detective with a gun and a grudge against lies.\n\nVince Corso started where every case starts: the bars. Lou poured whiskey and talked about the corrupt councilman's new operation, about money moving through Harbor District like blood through a bad heart."}, {'n': 3, 'title': "The Dame's Story", 'text': "She came back at midnight — the client, or the woman behind the client. Her story changed the way stories do when the truth puts on its coat. a broken watch, she admitted, was stolen. From the corrupt councilman.\n\nThey tailed the corrupt councilman's fixer to a warehouse on the waterfront. What they saw there turned Vince Corso's stomach: not just money, but the machinery of Harbor District's misery, oiled and humming.\n\nVince Corso took photographs with hands that didn't shake. Evidence. The one thing the corrupt councilman couldn't buy, beat, or bury. a broken watch had led here, as a broken watch always does, to the rotten center."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Squeeze', 'text': "Ruby the singer showed up bloody and grinning. 'They hit like accountants,' Ruby the singer said. Vince Corso felt the cold anger settle in, the kind that doesn't shout, just aims.\n\nVince Corso sat up all night with a broken watch and the photographs and the whiskey going untouched. By dawn there was a plan. It wasn't a good plan. It was the only plan.\n\nthe corrupt councilman squeezed back. First the office: tossed, the safe cracked, a broken watch's copy gone — but Vince Corso keeps the originals where no one looks. Then Ruby the singer: a warning delivered with fists in an alley off Harbor District."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Confrontation', 'text': "That night it rained again, washing Harbor District clean for an hour. Vince Corso stood at the window with a broken watch — the case closed, the ledger in the DA's hands. redemption costs extra, Vince Corso thought. Even here. Especially here.\n\nVince Corso walked into the corrupt councilman's office at noon, when Harbor District was bright and honest-looking, which made the lies inside seem darker. a broken watch went on the desk. The photographs went on the desk. The truth went on the desk.\n\nThe dame got her brother back and Vince Corso got the rest of the fee, which was more than the insulting original. She cried. Vince Corso didn't buy the tears but didn't charge for the shoulder, either."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Last Call', 'text': "Harbor District kept being Harbor District: rain, neon, lies, and now and then — now and then — a little justice, served neat, no ice. Vince Corso could live with that. Vince Corso had lived with worse.\n\nThe dame left town on the morning train, brother in tow, free and clean. She left Vince Corso a note: 'You were the only honest thing in it.' Vince Corso kept the note. Detectives keep everything.\n\nThe phone rang. Vince Corso picked it up. 'Vince Corso Investigations,' Vince Corso said, and listened, and smiled the thin smile of someone who knows exactly how much trouble costs. 'redemption costs extra. I'll take the case.'"}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

