en. From the Club Owner.\n\nThey tailed the Club Owner's fixer to a warehouse on the waterfront. What they saw there turned Sam Rourke's stomach: not just money, but the machinery of Harbor District's misery, oiled and humming."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Squeeze', 'text': "Ruby's club was next — a fire in the kitchen, a message in the smoke. Sam Rourke found her unharmed and furious, and that fury was worth ten guns. 'End this,' she said. Sam Rourke intended to.\n\nSam Rourke sat up all night with a bloodstained matchbook and the photographs and the whiskey going untouched. By dawn there was a plan. It wasn't a good plan. It was the only plan.\n\nthe Club Owner squeezed back. First the office: tossed, the safe cracked, a bloodstained matchbook's copy gone — but Sam Rourke keeps the originals where no one looks. Then Maggie the reporter: a warning delivered with fists in an alley off Harbor District."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Confrontation', 'text': "It went down the way it always goes: shouting, threats, a gun that Sam Rourke was faster than. When the police took the Club Owner out, Harbor District seemed to exhale, like a drunk finally telling the truth.\n\nSam Rourke walked into the Club Owner's office at noon, when Harbor District was bright and honest-looking, which made the lies inside seem darker. a bloodstained matchbook went on the desk. The photographs went on the desk. The truth went on the desk.\n\nThat night it rained again, washing Harbor District clean for an hour. Sam Rourke stood at the window with a bloodstained matchbook — the case closed, the ledger in the DA's hands. everyone has a price, and a line, Sam Rourke thought. Even here. Especially here."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Last Call', 'text': "Harbor District kept being Harbor District: rain, neon, lies, and now and then — now and then — a little justice, served neat, no ice. Sam Rourke could live with that. Sam Rourke had lived with worse.\n\nLou poured a double on the house. Ruby sang the song she only sings when she means it. Maggie the reporter's ribs were taped, but Maggie the reporter drank anyway, doctor's orders being for other people.\n\na bloodstained matchbook went into the office safe, next to the other honest things. Someday, Sam Rourke knew, someone would walk in with something like it, and it would start again. It always starts again."}]}

